# C++ style

Follow the [Google C++ Style Guide](https://google.github.io/styleguide/cppguide.html) in every first-party C and C++ file. Formatting is `.clang-format` (`BasedOnStyle: Google`). Lint is `.clang-tidy` (`google-*` plus Google naming).

- 2-space indent, 80 columns, no tabs. Attach braces to the line that opens the block. Put pointers and references on the left (`int* p`).
- Brace every `if`, `else`, `for`, `while`, and `do` body, including a single statement. Leave an `if` unbraced when a surrounding `#else` supplies the other branch, as in the `IEEE_ROUND_NEAREST` rounding paths.
- Types, functions, and enums are `PascalCase`. Enumerators and constants are `kPascalCase`. Variables and parameters are `snake_case`. Class data members end in `_`. Namespaces are lowercase. Macros are `UPPER_SNAKE`.
- Prefer `using` over `typedef`, `nullptr` over `NULL`, and `override` on virtual overrides in C++.
- No `using namespace` in headers. Constructors callable with one argument are `explicit`. Include the headers you use.
- Do not reorder includes. Configuration macros sit immediately above the header that reads them.

This tree is a C library. Keep the existing C API: `bid_*` functions and `BID_` / `DEC_` / `DPML_` / `UX_` / `IDEC_` macros and typedefs. Do not rename them, and do not rewrite C as C++ (`nullptr`, `using`, or `PascalCase` exports).

Do not add `Co-authored-by: Cursor` or any Cursor co-author trailer to a commit.

```cpp
class DecimalStatus {
 public:
  int flag_count() const { return flag_count_; }

 private:
  int flag_count_ = 0;
};
```
